--TEST--
An `exit` drains the `Core\Script::onExit` queue and runs no `finally`, so the hook observes the one ending nothing else can — `rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue` and `rule:observability/exit-hooks-run-after-the-ladder-before-teardown`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The ending this queue exists for. `exit` runs no `finally` — Novis skips it
// exactly as PHP does — so before this queue there was no way at all to run
// code at the ending a CLI program most wants to clean up after. Both halves
// are asserted here: the `finally` below does not print, and the hook does.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    string $reason = match ($report->reason()) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => "Normal",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => "ExitCall",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => "UncaughtThrow",
    };
    // `ExitCall` carries the suffix so the case never shares a spelling with
    // the keyword two lines below. The status is the one the call named, and
    // an `exit` has no error any more than a normal end does.
    echo "onExit: ", $reason, " ", $report->status(), " ",
        $report->error() == null ? "no error" : "an error", "\n";
});

try {
    echo "in the try\n";
    exit(0);
} finally {
    echo "this finally never runs\n";
}
--EXPECT--
in the try
onExit: ExitCall 0 no error
